    "label": "Prologue",
    "free": true,
    "text": "Before memory.\n\nBefore breath.\n\nBefore names.\n\nThere was agreement.\n\nNot all agreements are spoken. Some are simply understood, the way two rivers understand they are going to meet long before the land between them wears away. Some are witnessed. Some are carried, quietly and faithfully and at great cost, long after the ones who made them have forgotten they were ever made at all.\n\nThe House stood in silence.\n\nIt was not a house the way the word is used among the living — not brick, not timber, not a thing a man could build or burn. It was older than building. Its doors stretched farther than sight could follow, rank upon rank of them fading into a distance that was not quite distance, and no two of them were alike. Some were plain as a barn door. Some were carved with letters in tongues that had never been spoken aloud on the earth. Some seemed to have been made from stone, and some from wood so dark it drank the light, and a few — only a few — looked as though they had been shaped out of morning itself, and stood there glowing softly, patiently, the way a lamp burns in a window for someone who has not yet come home.\n\nSome had never once been opened.\n\nSome had been waiting longer than time had a way of counting.\n\nEach of them belonged to someone.\n\nThat was the first law of the House, and the deepest. No hand could force them. No strength could break them. No cunning could pick them, no fire could burn them, no lie could talk its way across their thresholds. They answered to one voice and one bearing only — to the one for whom, before memory and before breath, they had been prepared.\n\nA gathering stood before them.\n\nNo introductions were needed. No rank was announced. There was no throne, no herald, no lifting of any voice above another. Each of them knew why they had come, and each of them knew the others, and the knowing was so complete that speech, when it came, was almost an afterthought — a courtesy paid to the shape of things.\n\nThe silence was broken.\n\n\"His path has been measured.\"\n\n\"It has.\"\n\n\"The inheritance?\"\n\n\"It has been recorded.\"\n\n\"The keys?\"\n\n\"They are prepared.\"\n\nAnother silence. Longer. In that place a silence could carry more than any sentence, and this one carried something like grief, and something like resolve, and beneath both of those a patience so vast it had no floor.\n\nThen:\n\n\"The access?\"\n\n\"It will be enough.\"\n\nOne among the gathering did not answer.\n\nThe pause was almost invisible. Almost. But in the House, where nothing was hidden, even the smallest withholding stood out like a single dark door in a wall of burning ones.\n\nAnother voice spoke, and this voice was gentler than the rest, and sadder.\n\n\"He must choose.\"\n\n\"He will.\"\n\n\"He must forget.\"\n\nAgain — silence.\n\nNot everyone agreed.\n\nNothing more was said. In that place, disagreement did not need to be argued; it only needed to be seen, and it was seen, and it was allowed to stand, because even the House did not compel. The agreements were witnessed. The doors remained closed. One by one, the gathering departed, until the long corridors held only the soft, waiting light of the lamps that had been lit for the unarrived.\n\nOnly one remained.\n\nWatching.\n\nWaiting.\n\nAs though something, somewhere, had already begun.\n\nFar below — beneath the House, beneath the light, beneath the reach of any word that had been spoken there — in a hospital room on an ordinary night, in a country that kept its own kind of records, a child took his first breath.\n\nAnd somewhere in the House, without a hand to touch it, without a wind to move it, one of the doors trembled — only slightly, only once — the way a thing trembles when it recognizes, from very far off, the sound of a name it has been waiting its whole life to hear."
